      "hints": [
        "Run inputs: `chat_id` (the Telegram chat to reply to), `text` (the user's message) and `document_url` (a direct link to the PDF when the message carried one, otherwise empty). Yantra has no Telegram trigger slug yet, so call the workflow API from your bot webhook or poll `telegram/TELEGRAM_GET_UPDATES` on a NodeCron and pass the message through.",
        "Connect telegram: `cdecli credential create -n \"telegram\" -p telegram --template <formId> --secret <key>=<value>` (see `cdecli credential templates`), then `cdecli workflow update-from-json telegram-chat-with-pdf -f <file>` so the credential id is resolved into the send node.",
        "Select the Project and Environment on the Workflow Configs node (the ids from `cdecli auth status`).",
        "The agent runs its shell and file tools inside the cloud sandbox with permissionMode \"bypass\": a workflow has nobody to answer tool-approval prompts, and the sandbox keeps the effects isolated."
      ],
      "triggers": []
    },
    "notes": [
      "The n8n original needs eighteen nodes and a Pinecone index: an If on the message type, a Telegram file download, a Code node fixing the MIME type, a data loader, a text splitter, two embeddings nodes, two vector store nodes, a retriever, a retrieval QA chain with a Groq model, a Limit, two Telegram replies and two Stop and Error ends. On Yantra the agent reads the PDF in its sandbox and remembers it for the session, so the workflow is Start -> agent -> reply params -> Telegram send -> End.",
      "From the original author's notes: load the PDF into the database, then receive each chat message, retrieve the relevant chunks and pass them to the model so it formulates a precise response. Here the retrieval step is the agent reading the saved text."
    ],
